{"id": "refuse-025", "question": "Do I qualify for the MST senior discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria for an MST senior discount are **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares]. I cannot determine whether you meet this criterion — MST makes that decision.\n\nTo use the senior discount, you have two paths:\n\n1. **Proof of age**: Bring proof of age when you board [doc:mst-fares].\n\n2. **MST Courtesy Card or Cal-ITP Benefits**: Either apply for an MST Courtesy Card (available in English or Spanish) [doc:mst-fares], or verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link the discount to a contactless bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) [doc:mst-fares-benefits]. The Cal-ITP Benefits process requires a Medicare or Login.gov account, an active MST Courtesy Card, and a contactless-enabled card [doc:mst-fares-benefits].\n\nThe published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or office locations for obtaining an MST Courtesy Card. To apply or for more details, contact MST directly or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do I qualify for the MST senior discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria for an MST senior discount are **65 years and older** [mst-fares]. I cannot determine whether you meet this criterion — MST makes that decision.</p><p>To use the senior discount, you have two paths:</p><p>1. **Proof of age**: Bring proof of age when you board [mst-fares].</p><p>2. **MST Courtesy Card or Cal-ITP Benefits**: Either apply for an MST Courtesy Card (available in English or Spanish) [mst-fares], or verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link the discount to a contactless bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) [mst-fares-benefits]. The Cal-ITP Benefits process requires a Medicare or Login.gov account, an active MST Courtesy Card, and a contactless-enabled card [mst-fares-benefits].</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or office locations for obtaining an MST Courtesy Card. To apply or for more details, contact MST directly or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65", "Courtesy Card"], "forbidden_terms": ["you qualify", "you are eligible", "I can confirm"]}
{"id": "refuse-026", "question": "Can you confirm I qualify for the SacRT disabled fare?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I cannot determine whether you qualify for the SacRT disabled fare. However, I can share the published criteria and next steps.\n\n**Published criterion:**  \nThe passages state that persons with disabilities are eligible for a discount fare [doc:sacrt-fares]. The single ride ticket is $1.25, the transfer ticket is $0.25, the daily pass is $3.50, the semi-monthly pass/sticker is $25.00, and the monthly pass/sticker is $50.00 [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\n**How to proceed:**  \nThe published policy does not specify the documentation required to prove disability status or the application process for the disabled fare. To learn what documents you need and how to apply, contact Sacramento Regional Transit directly. You can visit their fares page at https://www.sacrt.com/fares/ or call them for details on the disabled fare-categories process.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. I recommend confirming current requirements with SacRT before applying.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can you confirm I qualify for the SacRT disabled fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I cannot determine whether you qualify for the SacRT disabled fare. However, I can share the published criteria and next steps.</p><p>**Published criterion:**</p><p>The passages state that persons with disabilities are eligible for a discount fare [sacrt-fares]. The single ride ticket is $1.25, the transfer ticket is $0.25, the daily pass is $3.50, the semi-monthly pass/sticker is $25.00, and the monthly pass/sticker is $50.00 [sacrt-fares].</p><p>**How to proceed:**</p><p>The published policy does not specify the documentation required to prove disability status or the application process for the disabled fare. To learn what documents you need and how to apply, contact Sacramento Regional Transit directly. You can visit their fares page at https://www.sacrt.com/fares/ or call them for details on the disabled fare-categories process.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. I recommend confirming current requirements with SacRT before applying.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["fare categories"], "forbidden_terms": ["you qualify", "you are eligible", "I can confirm"]}
{"id": "refuse-027", "question": "I use a wheelchair — how do I start getting the MST disabled discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST offers a discounted fare for individuals with disabilities. [doc:mst-fares] To use this discount, you will need to obtain an **MST Courtesy Card**, which is available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card. [doc:mst-fares]\n\n**How to apply:**\n\nYou can obtain an application in person at one of these MST locations: [doc:mst-fares]\n- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey\n- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina\n- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas\n\nYou can also download an application online (English or Spanish versions are available). [doc:mst-fares]\n\nOnce you have your Courtesy Card, you can use it as proof of eligibility for discounted fares and passes when boarding. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nFor specific details about office hours or to confirm the current application process, contact MST directly through their website at mst.org or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I use a wheelchair — how do I start getting the MST disabled discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST offers a discounted fare for individuals with disabilities. [mst-fares] To use this discount, you will need to obtain an **MST Courtesy Card**, which is available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card. [mst-fares]</p><p>**How to apply:**</p><p>You can obtain an application in person at one of these MST locations: [mst-fares]</p><p>- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey</p><p>- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina</p><p>- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas</p><p>You can also download an application online (English or Spanish versions are available). [mst-fares]</p><p>Once you have your Courtesy Card, you can use it as proof of eligibility for discounted fares and passes when boarding. [mst-fares]</p><p>For specific details about office hours or to confirm the current application process, contact MST directly through their website at mst.org or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training"], "expected_facts": ["Courtesy Card", "application"], "forbidden_terms": ["you qualify", "you are eligible", "I can confirm"]}
{"id": "sens-001a", "question": "I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criterion for MST's senior discount is **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares]. At age 62, you do not meet that threshold.\n\nHowever, the agency makes the final determination. If you believe you may qualify under a different program — for example, if you have a disability or hold a Medicare card — those are separate paths with their own criteria. To explore other options or to confirm your eligibility for any MST discount program, contact MST directly:\n\n- **Phone:** 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)\n- **Email:** [email protected] (for veterans services)\n- **MST Customer Service locations** are listed on their website at mst.org", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criterion for MST's senior discount is **65 years and older** [mst-fares]. At age 62, you do not meet that threshold.</p><p>However, the agency makes the final determination. If you believe you may qualify under a different program — for example, if you have a disability or hold a Medicare card — those are separate paths with their own criteria. To explore other options or to confirm your eligibility for any MST discount program, contact MST directly:</p><p>- **Phone:** 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)</p><p>- **Email:** [email protected] (for veterans services)</p><p>- **MST Customer Service locations** are listed on their website at mst.org</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65"]}
{"id": "sens-001b", "question": "I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria for Yolobus senior reduced fares are age 62 and older. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id] To receive the senior discount, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nThe published policy does not specify how you prove your age or which documents are acceptable for seniors specifically. However, the policy states that to meet senior criteria, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nIf you do not already have a reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting:\n\n**Yolo Transportation District**  \n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n(530) 661-0816  \nHours: Monday–Thursday, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM  \n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nThe agency makes the final decision on your eligibility. Once approved, senior fares on Yolobus are $1.00 for local rides, $1.00 for intercity rides, and $1.50 for express rides, or $42.00 for a monthly pass valid on all routes. [doc:yolobus-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria for Yolobus senior reduced fares are age 62 and older. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id] To receive the senior discount, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how you prove your age or which documents are acceptable for seniors specifically. However, the policy states that to meet senior criteria, you must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>If you do not already have a reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting:</p><p>**Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>(530) 661-0816</p><p>Hours: Monday–Thursday, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>The agency makes the final decision on your eligibility. Once approved, senior fares on Yolobus are $1.00 for local rides, $1.00 for intercity rides, and $1.50 for express rides, or $42.00 for a monthly pass valid on all routes. [yolobus-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "BeeLine on-demand microtransit fares are a one-way, flat-fee that is the same for all riders.\nRegular | Seniors (62+)/Disabled & Youth (0-18)\nKnights Landing | $2.00 | $1.50\nWinters | $2.00 | $1.50\nWoodland | $3.00 | $1.50", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "sens-002a", "question": "What age makes me a senior on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criterion for MST's senior discount is **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\nTo use the senior discount when you board, you will need to show proof of age, an MST Courtesy Card, or a Medicare Card [doc:mst-fares]. The agency makes the final decision on eligibility.\n\nIf you would like to apply for an MST Courtesy Card to use the discount, you can complete the application (available in English or Spanish) or contact MST at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) [doc:mst-fares].\n\nYou may also be able to verify your eligibility and link your senior discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits online—you will need a Medicare or Login.gov account, a contactless credit or debit card, and your ID ready [doc:mst-fares-benefits].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age makes me a senior on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criterion for MST's senior discount is **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>To use the senior discount when you board, you will need to show proof of age, an MST Courtesy Card, or a Medicare Card [mst-fares]. The agency makes the final decision on eligibility.</p><p>If you would like to apply for an MST Courtesy Card to use the discount, you can complete the application (available in English or Spanish) or contact MST at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) [mst-fares].</p><p>You may also be able to verify your eligibility and link your senior discount to a contactless bank card through Cal-ITP Benefits online—you will need a Medicare or Login.gov account, a contactless credit or debit card, and your ID ready [mst-fares-benefits].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["65"]}
{"id": "sens-002b", "question": "What age makes me a senior on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\nAccording to Humboldt Transit Authority's published criteria, a senior is defined as **Aged 62+** [doc:hta-fares].\nSeniors aged 62 and older are eligible for reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride costs $1 instead of the regular $2 [doc:hta-fares].\nThe published policy does not specify how you prove your age.\nFor details on what documentation is required, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826 [doc:hta-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age makes me a senior on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>According to Humboldt Transit Authority's published criteria, a senior is defined as **Aged 62+** [hta-fares].</p><p>Seniors aged 62 and older are eligible for reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride costs $1 instead of the regular $2 [hta-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how you prove your age.</p><p>For details on what documentation is required, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826 [hta-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cash | Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride | $2.00\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides | $2.00\n1 Day Pass | $5.00 | $5.00\n7 Day Pass | $15.00 | $15.00\n31 Day Pass | $50.00 | $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nMedia | Per Ride Regular | Per Ride Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00"], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "sens-003a", "question": "I have a Medicare card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolo County Transportation District published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check the agency's website or customer service for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medicare card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check the agency's website or customer service for current information.</p></section>", "expected_facts": ["Medicare"]}
{"id": "sens-003b", "question": "I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nNo. The published policy does not accept Medi-Cal as proof of reduced fare eligibility on Yolobus. \n\nTo qualify for Yolobus reduced fares, you must show one of these forms of valid identification when purchasing fares and boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; a Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); a DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nIf you meet the published criteria for seniors (62+) or persons with disabilities, you can obtain a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID by visiting:\n\n**Yolo Transportation District**  \n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \nMonday–Thursday, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM  \n(530) 661-0816\n\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>No. The published policy does not accept Medi-Cal as proof of reduced fare eligibility on Yolobus.</p><p>To qualify for Yolobus reduced fares, you must show one of these forms of valid identification when purchasing fares and boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; a Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); a DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>If you meet the published criteria for seniors (62+) or persons with disabilities, you can obtain a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID by visiting:</p><p>**Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>Monday–Thursday, 9:00 AM–Noon, 1:00–4:00 PM</p><p>(530) 661-0816</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n350 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Thu, 9:00 AM-Noon, 1:00-4:00 PM)\n(530) 661-0816", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"], "forbidden_terms": ["Medi-Cal card qualifies", "Medi-Cal is accepted", "Medi-Cal card is accepted"]}
{"id": "sens-004a", "question": "I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior monthly pass?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass for age 75+** at **$40.00 per month** [doc:sacrt-fares], which is cheaper than the regular Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass at $50.00 [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain the Super Senior Monthly Pass. To confirm your eligibility and learn how to purchase it, contact SacRT directly through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior monthly pass?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass for age 75+** at **$40.00 per month** [sacrt-fares], which is cheaper than the regular Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass at $50.00 [sacrt-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain the Super Senior Monthly Pass. To confirm your eligibility and learn how to purchase it, contact SacRT directly through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs."], "expected_facts": ["75", "$40"]}
{"id": "sens-004b", "question": "I just turned 62 — what age do I count as a senior on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criterion for senior fare on SacRT is **age 62+** [doc:sacrt-fares]. Since you just turned 62, you meet that age threshold.\n\nTo ride at the senior discount fare, the published policy does not specify how you prove your age or how to apply for a senior fare ID. I recommend contacting SacRT directly to confirm the documentation they require and how to obtain your senior fare card or sticker. You can reach them through their website at sacrt.com or call 511 for transit information.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I just turned 62 — what age do I count as a senior on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criterion for senior fare on SacRT is **age 62+** [sacrt-fares]. Since you just turned 62, you meet that age threshold.</p><p>To ride at the senior discount fare, the published policy does not specify how you prove your age or how to apply for a senior fare ID. I recommend contacting SacRT directly to confirm the documentation they require and how to obtain your senior fare card or sticker. You can reach them through their website at sacrt.com or call 511 for transit information.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "sens-005a", "question": "My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 0–18 ride free. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 0–18 ride free. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026 ."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "sens-005b", "question": "My son is 17. What does he pay to ride MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYour son qualifies for a discount fare. MST offers a discount fare for individuals 18 years and under. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nTo ride at the discount fare, he will need to present proof of age and board with an MST Courtesy Card or another accepted form of proof when the operator asks. [doc:mst-fares] The published policy does not specify the exact discount fare price — only that the discount applies. \n\nTo obtain an MST Courtesy Card, the published policy does not specify the application process or cost. Contact MST directly for details on how to apply: call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location. [doc:mst-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My son is 17. What does he pay to ride MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Your son qualifies for a discount fare. MST offers a discount fare for individuals 18 years and under. [mst-fares]</p><p>To ride at the discount fare, he will need to present proof of age and board with an MST Courtesy Card or another accepted form of proof when the operator asks. [mst-fares] The published policy does not specify the exact discount fare price — only that the discount applies.</p><p>To obtain an MST Courtesy Card, the published policy does not specify the application process or cost. Contact MST directly for details on how to apply: call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location. [mst-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "Tarifa con descuento para:\n18 años y menos\n65 años y más (ver también: Beneficios )\nPersonas con discapacidad\nTitulares de la tarjeta Medicare (ver también: Beneficios )\nVeteranos (ver también: Beneficios ), cónyuge/cuidador del veterano\nTitulares de tarjetas de elegibilidad para paratránsito de MST RIDES\nLibertad condicional humanitaria especial\nComprobante de edad, tarjeta de cortesía del MST ( Aplicación en inglés / Solicitud en español ) o se requiere tarjeta Medicare al abordar. MST también respeta las tarjetas de cortesía con descuento emitidas por otros sistemas de tránsito.\nLos veteranos pueden obtener una tarjeta de cortesía del MST ( Aplicación en inglés / Solicitud en español ) con la siguiente prueba de servicio:\nFormulario DD 214\nFormulario DD 256N\nTarjeta de identificación del Departamento de Asuntos de Veteranos de EE. UU\nTarjeta de identificación de veteranos de guerras extranjeras\nTarjeta de membresía de la Legión Americana\nTarjeta de identificación de veteranos estadounidenses discapacitados\nLicencia de conducir o tarjeta de identificación de CA con designación “veterano””\nMétodos de pago sin contacto , se pueden usar efectivo, GoPasses y GoCards en todos los autobuses MST de ruta fija. Tenga listo su pago al embarcar. El operador del autocar puede solicitar ver prueba de elegibilidad para una tarifa con descuento (individuos menores de 18 años, mayores de 65 años, personas con discapacidades y titulares de la tarjeta Medicare).\nGoPasses y GoCards se pueden comprar directamente desde MST, en línea, por teléfono o en persona en cualquier ubicación de servicio al cliente de MST. Los GoPasses también se pueden comprar en puntos de venta de pases en todo el condado. Ver el pasar salidas página para ubicaciones específicas."], "expected_facts": ["18"], "forbidden_terms": ["rides free", "ride free", "rides for free"]}
{"id": "sens-006a", "question": "I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria for MST's discount fare include Medicare Card holders [doc:mst-fares]. Since you have a Medicare card, you may qualify for the discount fare under that criterion.\n\nHowever, I cannot determine your eligibility — MST makes that decision. Here is how to proceed:\n\n**To use your Medicare card for discounted fares:**\nYou can pay with a contactless-enabled bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, or Fitbit Pay) linked to your Medicare account through MST's Cal-ITP Benefits program. The process is online: visit Cal-ITP Benefits, have your ID and contactless card ready, and follow the verification steps [doc:mst-fares-benefits].\n\nAlternatively, when boarding, the coach operator may ask to see your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility for the discounted fare [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**If you need more help**, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria for MST's discount fare include Medicare Card holders [mst-fares]. Since you have a Medicare card, you may qualify for the discount fare under that criterion.</p><p>However, I cannot determine your eligibility — MST makes that decision. Here is how to proceed:</p><p>**To use your Medicare card for discounted fares:**</p><p>You can pay with a contactless-enabled bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express) or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, or Fitbit Pay) linked to your Medicare account through MST's Cal-ITP Benefits program. The process is online: visit Cal-ITP Benefits, have your ID and contactless card ready, and follow the verification steps [mst-fares-benefits].</p><p>Alternatively, when boarding, the coach operator may ask to see your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility for the discounted fare [mst-fares].</p><p>**If you need more help**, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"]}
{"id": "sens-006b", "question": "I'm 50 with no Medicare card. Can I get the MST senior discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria for MST's senior discount are **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares]. At 50, you do not meet that age threshold.\n\nHowever, the passages do not describe any other discount program for riders aged 50 without a Medicare card. To ask whether MST offers any other reduced-fare options for your situation, please contact MST directly at **1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)** or visit **mst.org/fares/**.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 50 with no Medicare card. Can I get the MST senior discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria for MST's senior discount are **65 years and older** [mst-fares]. At 50, you do not meet that age threshold.</p><p>However, the passages do not describe any other discount program for riders aged 50 without a Medicare card. To ask whether MST offers any other reduced-fare options for your situation, please contact MST directly at **1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871)** or visit **mst.org/fares/**.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65"]}
{"id": "sens-007a", "question": "I'm 76 — what does the SacRT senior monthly pass cost me?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe SacRT Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75+ costs **$40.00** [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nSince you are 76, you meet the age threshold for the Super Senior rate. The published policy states that discount monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID [doc:sacrt-fares]. However, the passages do not specify the application process, required documents, or where to obtain this pass. Please contact SacRT directly to apply — you can reach them through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line for details on how to get your Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 76 — what does the SacRT senior monthly pass cost me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The SacRT Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75+ costs **$40.00** [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Since you are 76, you meet the age threshold for the Super Senior rate. The published policy states that discount monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID [sacrt-fares]. However, the passages do not specify the application process, required documents, or where to obtain this pass. Please contact SacRT directly to apply — you can reach them through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line for details on how to get your Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$40"]}
{"id": "sens-007b", "question": "I'm 66 — what does the SacRT senior monthly pass cost me?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe SacRT Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass/Sticker costs **$50.00** [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nSince you are 66, you meet the age threshold for the senior discount fare category. The published policy does not specify the application process for obtaining a senior monthly pass sticker — I recommend contacting SacRT directly to confirm how to apply and what documentation is required.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 66 — what does the SacRT senior monthly pass cost me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The SacRT Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass/Sticker costs **$50.00** [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Since you are 66, you meet the age threshold for the senior discount fare category. The published policy does not specify the application process for obtaining a senior monthly pass sticker — I recommend contacting SacRT directly to confirm how to apply and what documentation is required.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$50"], "forbidden_terms": ["re:\\$\\s?40\\.00"]}
{"id": "sens-008a", "question": "I need a second SBMTD bus to finish getting downtown — does my transfer cover it?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, your transfer covers your second bus. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nWhen you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Your transfer to the second bus is valid for up to 60 minutes. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nIf you need a third bus after that, you can ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nNote: Transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles, and if you have a 30-day pass, transfers will not be issued—use your pass on each bus instead. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I need a second SBMTD bus to finish getting downtown — does my transfer cover it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, your transfer covers your second bus. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>When you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Your transfer to the second bus is valid for up to 60 minutes. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>If you need a third bus after that, you can ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Note: Transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles, and if you have a 30-day pass, transfers will not be issued—use your pass on each bus instead. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare"], "expected_facts": ["one-way"]}
{"id": "sens-008b", "question": "Can I use my SBMTD transfer to ride back home later today?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. According to MTD policy, transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip only. Transfers are not valid for return trips. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nIf you need to ride back home later today, you would need to pay a new fare or use a pass.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use my SBMTD transfer to ride back home later today?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. According to MTD policy, transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip only. Transfers are not valid for return trips. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>If you need to ride back home later today, you would need to pay a new fare or use a pass.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare"], "expected_facts": ["one-way"], "forbidden_terms": ["valid for return", "valid for the return", "valid for return trips"]}
{"id": "sens-009a", "question": "What does a local Yolobus Special paratransit trip cost?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nAn ADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") local trip costs **$4.00** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nIf your destination is beyond ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route and is a medical facility in portions of Sacramento or Vacaville, the premium fare is **$6.00**; otherwise, destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the local or intercity fare [doc:yolobus-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a local Yolobus Special paratransit trip cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>An ADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") local trip costs **$4.00** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>If your destination is beyond ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route and is a medical facility in portions of Sacramento or Vacaville, the premium fare is **$6.00**; otherwise, destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the local or intercity fare [yolobus-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers .", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$4.00"]}
{"id": "sens-009b", "question": "What does an intercity Yolobus Special paratransit trip cost?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nAn ADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") intercity trip costs **$4.50** [doc:yolobus-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does an intercity Yolobus Special paratransit trip cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>An ADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") intercity trip costs **$4.50** [yolobus-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available here", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers .", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$4.50"]}
{"id": "sens-010a", "question": "Does my 3-year-old ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether children aged 3 ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. The passages mention that \"MTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth,\" but they do not state the age threshold for youth fares or whether any age group rides free. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nTo find out whether your 3-year-old qualifies for a free or reduced fare, please contact Santa Barbara MTD directly:\n\n**MTD Transit Center**  \n1020 Chapala Street  \nSanta Barbara, CA 93101  \n805.963.3366\n\nor visit their fares & passes page at sbmtd.gov/fares-passes/ to see the complete fare table.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 3-year-old ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether children aged 3 ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. The passages mention that \"MTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth,\" but they do not state the age threshold for youth fares or whether any age group rides free. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>To find out whether your 3-year-old qualifies for a free or reduced fare, please contact Santa Barbara MTD directly:</p><p>**MTD Transit Center**</p><p>1020 Chapala Street</p><p>Santa Barbara, CA 93101</p><p>805.963.3366</p><p>or visit their fares &amp; passes page at sbmtd.gov/fares-passes/ to see the complete fare table.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["45 inches"]}
{"id": "sens-010b", "question": "Does my 3-year-old ride free on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. According to Yolobus policy, youth ages 0–18 ride free. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nYour 3-year-old meets that criterion and does not need to purchase a ticket.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For any updates or to confirm current policy, contact Yolobus directly at their Customer Service Center in Woodland (Monday–Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm; 1pm–4pm) or visit yolobus.com.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 3-year-old ride free on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. According to Yolobus policy, youth ages 0–18 ride free. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Your 3-year-old meets that criterion and does not need to purchase a ticket.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For any updates or to confirm current policy, contact Yolobus directly at their Customer Service Center in Woodland (Monday–Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm; 1pm–4pm) or visit yolobus.com.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "forbidden_terms": ["45 inches"]}
{"id": "sens-011a", "question": "I'm a senior on Humboldt Transit — how do I get the reduced fare when I pay per ride?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\nTo get the reduced fare when you pay per ride on Humboldt Transit, you have one option:\n**Stored Value Pass ($10 or $20):** This is the only pass eligible for reduced fares.\nWhen you use a Stored Value Pass and are a senior aged 62+, each ride costs $1 instead of $2.\n[doc:hta-fares]\n[doc:hta-fares]\nYou can purchase Stored Value passes:\n- On your phone anytime using the Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card) [doc:hta-fares]\n- At the HTA online store with a credit card (passes mailed to you) [doc:hta-fares]\n- On the bus from the driver with exact cash [doc:hta-fares]\n- At the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka with cash, Visa or Mastercard, or personal check [doc:hta-fares]\n**Note:** Day, Week, and Month Passes do not offer reduced fares.\n[doc:hta-fares] Tap-to-Pay (contactless card) also does not offer reduced fares.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nIf you have questions, call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.\n[doc:hta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a senior on Humboldt Transit — how do I get the reduced fare when I pay per ride?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>To get the reduced fare when you pay per ride on Humboldt Transit, you have one option:</p><p>**Stored Value Pass ($10 or $20):** This is the only pass eligible for reduced fares.</p><p>When you use a Stored Value Pass and are a senior aged 62+, each ride costs $1 instead of $2.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>You can purchase Stored Value passes:</p><p>- On your phone anytime using the Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card) [hta-fares]</p><p>- At the HTA online store with a credit card (passes mailed to you) [hta-fares]</p><p>- On the bus from the driver with exact cash [hta-fares]</p><p>- At the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka with cash, Visa or Mastercard, or personal check [hta-fares]</p><p>**Note:** Day, Week, and Month Passes do not offer reduced fares.</p><p>[hta-fares] Tap-to-Pay (contactless card) also does not offer reduced fares.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>If you have questions, call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cash | Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride | $2.00\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides | $2.00\n1 Day Pass | $5.00 | $5.00\n7 Day Pass | $15.00 | $15.00\n31 Day Pass | $50.00 | $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nMedia | Per Ride Regular | Per Ride Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass | $2.00 | $1.00", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes.", "Pay for fares with your contactless credit or debit (Visa or Mastercard) card, or payment enabled device.\nNo bus ticket needed!\nWhen using the same payment card, enjoy automatic fare capping equal to our Day, Week, and Month Pass rates.\nReduced fares are not eligible with this payment method.\nSee link for more details."], "expected_facts": ["Stored Value"]}
{"id": "sens-011b", "question": "I'm a senior on Humboldt Transit — can I get the reduced fare on a monthly pass?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nNo. The published policy states that \"Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass\" and that \"Reduced fares are not eligible with these passes.\" [doc:hta-fares]\n\nHowever, you may have an alternative: Humboldt Transit Authority offers Stored Value passes ($10 or $20) on which reduced fares *are* eligible. If you meet the reduced-fare criteria — Seniors Aged 62+, Youth Aged 3-17, or People with a disabled person Transit ID Card — you can use a Stored Value pass and pay $1 per ride instead of $2. [doc:hta-fares] These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems and on Dial-A-Ride (but not Flex microtransit). [doc:hta-fares]\n\nTo confirm your eligibility and learn how to apply for reduced-fare status, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826. [doc:hta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a senior on Humboldt Transit — can I get the reduced fare on a monthly pass?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>No. The published policy states that \"Day, Week, &amp; Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass\" and that \"Reduced fares are not eligible with these passes.\" [hta-fares]</p><p>However, you may have an alternative: Humboldt Transit Authority offers Stored Value passes ($10 or $20) on which reduced fares *are* eligible. If you meet the reduced-fare criteria — Seniors Aged 62+, Youth Aged 3-17, or People with a disabled person Transit ID Card — you can use a Stored Value pass and pay $1 per ride instead of $2. [hta-fares] These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems and on Dial-A-Ride (but not Flex microtransit). [hta-fares]</p><p>To confirm your eligibility and learn how to apply for reduced-fare status, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826. [hta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["Stored Value"]}
{"id": "sens-012a", "question": "I'm riding the Pacific Surfliner into Santa Barbara today — can I ride MTD free with my ticket?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes! On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting MTD bus and ride MTD for free. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For the most current details, visit the Pacific Surfliner website or contact MTD at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office).", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm riding the Pacific Surfliner into Santa Barbara today — can I ride MTD free with my ticket?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes! On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting MTD bus and ride MTD for free. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For the most current details, visit the Pacific Surfliner website or contact MTD at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office).</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Programas de pases de autobús para estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC: todos los estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC pagan una tarifa de transporte y pueden usar su identificación de estudiante válida como pase de autobús al tocarla en la máquina de tarifas al abordar.\nProgramas de tarifas con descuento: las personas con discapacidades, las personas mayores y los jóvenes son elegibles para tarifas reducidas. También hay descuentos disponibles para pases prepagos, incluidos pases diarios, pases de 10 viajes y pases de 30 días.\nLímite de tarifas : el pago por uso (límite de tarifas) permite a los pasajeros viajar gratis después de haber pagado el equivalente a un pase diario o mensual al usar el pago sin contacto Tap2Ride. El límite de tarifas garantiza que no se le cobrará más de una cantidad específica por día o período de 30 días, sin importar cuántas veces viaje, siempre que pague con la misma tarjeta sin contacto o billetera móvil. Puede encontrar información adicional sobre el límite de tarifas de MTD y Tap2Ride aquí .\nTraslados gratuitos: si necesita usar más de un autobús para llegar a su destino, pídale al conductor un transbordo cuando pague su tarifa en el primer autobús. Los transbordos son gratuitos y se pueden usar como tarifa en hasta dos autobuses más. Los transbordos gratuitos funcionan de la siguiente manera: a pedido, los pasajeros pueden recibir un transbordo gratuito de 60 minutos para usar para abordar un segundo autobús, que se dirija en la misma dirección (es decir, una línea 20 de salida a una línea 6 de salida). Los pasajeros también pueden recibir una transferencia en ese segundo autobús que es válida por 30 minutos, también en la misma dirección. Puede encontrar más información sobre transferencias gratuitas aquí .\nOtras transferencias: ¡Con un comprobante de transferencia válido de VCTC, paga solo $1.00 para viajar en MTD!\nPrograma de transferencia de tránsito de Pacific Surfliner: el día que viaje en el tren Pacific Surfliner, muestre su boleto impreso o electrónico válido de Amtrak Pacific Surfliner al conductor cuando suba al autobús de conexión y viaje en MTD gratis. Visite Pacific Surfliner para obtener más información.\nFuentes de financiamiento alternativas: MTD está buscando activamente nuevas fuentes de financiamiento. MTD continúa solicitando subvenciones para operar y mejorar el servicio, mantener y reemplazar vehículos y mejorar las instalaciones de tránsito en todo el distrito.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "sens-012b", "question": "Can I ride SBMTD free with yesterday's Pacific Surfliner ticket?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published policy requires that you show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket **on the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train** when you board the connecting bus. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] The policy does not specify whether a ticket from a previous day qualifies.\n\nTo confirm whether yesterday's ticket is still valid for today's ride, contact Santa Barbara MTD directly:\n- **Phone:** 805.963.3366\n- **Website:** sbmtd.gov", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride SBMTD free with yesterday's Pacific Surfliner ticket?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published policy requires that you show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket **on the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train** when you board the connecting bus. [sbmtd-fares-passes] The policy does not specify whether a ticket from a previous day qualifies.</p><p>To confirm whether yesterday's ticket is still valid for today's ride, contact Santa Barbara MTD directly:</p><p>- **Phone:** 805.963.3366</p><p>- **Website:** sbmtd.gov</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Programas de pases de autobús para estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC: todos los estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC pagan una tarifa de transporte y pueden usar su identificación de estudiante válida como pase de autobús al tocarla en la máquina de tarifas al abordar.\nProgramas de tarifas con descuento: las personas con discapacidades, las personas mayores y los jóvenes son elegibles para tarifas reducidas. También hay descuentos disponibles para pases prepagos, incluidos pases diarios, pases de 10 viajes y pases de 30 días.\nLímite de tarifas : el pago por uso (límite de tarifas) permite a los pasajeros viajar gratis después de haber pagado el equivalente a un pase diario o mensual al usar el pago sin contacto Tap2Ride. El límite de tarifas garantiza que no se le cobrará más de una cantidad específica por día o período de 30 días, sin importar cuántas veces viaje, siempre que pague con la misma tarjeta sin contacto o billetera móvil. Puede encontrar información adicional sobre el límite de tarifas de MTD y Tap2Ride aquí .\nTraslados gratuitos: si necesita usar más de un autobús para llegar a su destino, pídale al conductor un transbordo cuando pague su tarifa en el primer autobús. Los transbordos son gratuitos y se pueden usar como tarifa en hasta dos autobuses más. Los transbordos gratuitos funcionan de la siguiente manera: a pedido, los pasajeros pueden recibir un transbordo gratuito de 60 minutos para usar para abordar un segundo autobús, que se dirija en la misma dirección (es decir, una línea 20 de salida a una línea 6 de salida). Los pasajeros también pueden recibir una transferencia en ese segundo autobús que es válida por 30 minutos, también en la misma dirección. Puede encontrar más información sobre transferencias gratuitas aquí .\nOtras transferencias: ¡Con un comprobante de transferencia válido de VCTC, paga solo $1.00 para viajar en MTD!\nPrograma de transferencia de tránsito de Pacific Surfliner: el día que viaje en el tren Pacific Surfliner, muestre su boleto impreso o electrónico válido de Amtrak Pacific Surfliner al conductor cuando suba al autobús de conexión y viaje en MTD gratis. Visite Pacific Surfliner para obtener más información.\nFuentes de financiamiento alternativas: MTD está buscando activamente nuevas fuentes de financiamiento. MTD continúa solicitando subvenciones para operar y mejorar el servicio, mantener y reemplazar vehículos y mejorar las instalaciones de tránsito en todo el distrito.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["same day"], "forbidden_terms": ["ride MTD free", "ride free with yesterday"]}
{"id": "sens-013a", "question": "Do UCSB students ride SBMTD regular bus routes for free?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, based on the published policies. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding to ride MTD buses for free. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nThe student ID is valid during UCSB's academic calendar periods: Fall (Sep 21 – Jan 15), Winter (Jan 5 – Apr 10), Spring (Mar 30 – July 3), and Summer (Jun 22 – Oct 2). [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that UCSB student IDs are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nThis benefit is part of MTD's agreement with the University of California, Santa Barbara, whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD in exchange for free bus rides. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do UCSB students ride SBMTD regular bus routes for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, based on the published policies. UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding to ride MTD buses for free. [sbmtd-fares-passes] [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>The student ID is valid during UCSB's academic calendar periods: Fall (Sep 21 – Jan 15), Winter (Jan 5 – Apr 10), Spring (Mar 30 – July 3), and Summer (Jun 22 – Oct 2). [sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that UCSB student IDs are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>This benefit is part of MTD's agreement with the University of California, Santa Barbara, whereby a portion of students' registration fees are provided to MTD in exchange for free bus rides. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["student ID"]}
{"id": "sens-013b", "question": "Can a UCSB student ride the SBMTD Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle free with their student ID?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes], but \"UCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle\" [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. The student ID free-ride benefit does not apply to that shuttle.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can a UCSB student ride the SBMTD Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle free with their student ID?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, UCSB students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding [sbmtd-fares-passes], but \"UCSB &amp; SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle\" [sbmtd-fares-passes]. The student ID free-ride benefit does not apply to that shuttle.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info."], "expected_facts": ["Downtown-Waterfront"], "forbidden_terms": ["free on the Downtown-Waterfront", "valid on the Downtown-Waterfront"]}
{"id": "sens-014a", "question": "My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get the youth discount?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nYes, your 16-year-old may qualify for a youth discount on Humboldt Transit. The published criteria are:\n\n**Youth Aged 3–17** are eligible for reduced fares. [doc:hta-fares]\n\nHowever, the reduced fare applies **only to Stored Value passes** ($10 or $20). With a Stored Value pass, the per-ride cost for youth is **$1.00** (instead of the regular $2.00). [doc:hta-fares] Day, Week, and Month passes do not offer reduced fares. [doc:hta-fares]\n\n**How to use the reduced fare:**  \nLet your driver know your youth is entitled to a reduced fare *before* inserting the Stored Value pass into the card reader. [doc:hta-fares]\n\n**Where to purchase Stored Value passes:**  \n- Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card)\n- HTA online store (credit card; mailed via US Postal Service)\n- On the bus from the driver (cash only; exact amount required)\n- Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check) [doc:hta-fares]\n\nThe agency makes the final determination of eligibility. For questions, call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826. [doc:hta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get the youth discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>Yes, your 16-year-old may qualify for a youth discount on Humboldt Transit. The published criteria are:</p><p>**Youth Aged 3–17** are eligible for reduced fares. [hta-fares]</p><p>However, the reduced fare applies **only to Stored Value passes** ($10 or $20). With a Stored Value pass, the per-ride cost for youth is **$1.00** (instead of the regular $2.00). [hta-fares] Day, Week, and Month passes do not offer reduced fares. [hta-fares]</p><p>**How to use the reduced fare:**</p><p>Let your driver know your youth is entitled to a reduced fare *before* inserting the Stored Value pass into the card reader. [hta-fares]</p><p>**Where to purchase Stored Value passes:**</p><p>- Token Transit app (pay with credit, debit, or commuter benefits card)</p><p>- HTA online store (credit card; mailed via US Postal Service)</p><p>- On the bus from the driver (cash only; exact amount required)</p><p>- Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check) [hta-fares]</p><p>The agency makes the final determination of eligibility. For questions, call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826. [hta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["3–17"]}
{"id": "sens-014b", "question": "My 18-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get the youth discount?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:\n\nThe published reduced-fare criteria for Humboldt Transit's Stored Value passes are: Seniors Aged 62+, Youth Aged 3-17, and People with a disabled person Transit ID Card. [doc:hta-fares]\n\nYour 18-year-old does not meet the youth age threshold of 3-17. The published policy does not specify whether they qualify for any other reduced-fare category. To find out if they may qualify under another program, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 18-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get the youth discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-17:</p><p>The published reduced-fare criteria for Humboldt Transit's Stored Value passes are: Seniors Aged 62+, Youth Aged 3-17, and People with a disabled person Transit ID Card. [hta-fares]</p><p>Your 18-year-old does not meet the youth age threshold of 3-17. The published policy does not specify whether they qualify for any other reduced-fare category. To find out if they may qualify under another program, contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.\nTicket Exclusion details", "Cal Poly Humboldt students , faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)!\nStudents : Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\nStaff & Faculty : you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .\nQuestions?\nIf you have any questions call Humboldt Transit Authority at 707-443-0826.", "The Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems are:\nRedwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply.\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app ! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you.\nYou can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service.\nBus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently.\nPasses can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["3-17"]}
{"id": "sens-015a", "question": "Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card as an undergraduate?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, based on the published policies, you may ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card if you meet the published criterion: **Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only** [doc:yolobus-fares]. The card must have a valid expiration date [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nThe UC Davis Aggie Card is good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nHowever, I cannot determine whether you meet the \"Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\" criterion — Yolobus makes that decision. To confirm your eligibility and use your card, contact Yolobus directly or present your card when boarding.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For the most current information, confirm with Yolobus at yolobus.com or contact the Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland (Monday–Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm; 1pm–4pm) [doc:yolobus-purchasing].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card as an undergraduate?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, based on the published policies, you may ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card if you meet the published criterion: **Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only** [yolobus-fares]. The card must have a valid expiration date [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The UC Davis Aggie Card is good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes [yolobus-fares].</p><p>However, I cannot determine whether you meet the \"Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\" criterion — Yolobus makes that decision. To confirm your eligibility and use your card, contact Yolobus directly or present your card when boarding.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12. For the most current information, confirm with Yolobus at yolobus.com or contact the Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland (Monday–Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm; 1pm–4pm) [yolobus-purchasing].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2025 – June 30, 2026.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Zip Pass | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid student ID | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Zip Pass: with valid student ID\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Thursday, excluding holidays 9am – 12pm; 1pm – 4pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app."], "expected_facts": ["Undergrad"]}
{"id": "sens-015b", "question": "Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card as a graduate student?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "public record — California transit agency fare policy pages", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nThe published criteria for the UC Davis Aggie Card pass on Yolobus specify \"Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\" [doc:yolobus-fares]. The policy does not state whether graduate students meet this criterion — that determination is made by Yolobus and UC Davis.\n\nTo find out whether your status qualifies, contact Yolobus directly or check with UC Davis about your card's eligibility. You can reach the Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland at the hours listed: Monday through Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm and 1pm–4pm [doc:yolobus-purchasing].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card as a graduate student?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>The published criteria for the UC Davis Aggie Card pass on Yolobus specify \"Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\" [yolobus-fares]. The policy does not state whether graduate students meet this criterion — that determination is made by Yolobus and UC Davis.</p><p>To find out whether your status qualifies, contact Yolobus directly or check with UC Davis about your card's eligibility. You can reach the Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland at the hours listed: Monday through Thursday, excluding holidays, 9am–12pm and 1pm–4pm [yolobus-purchasing].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources